## Prose

Line breaks in prose carry meaning; they are not visual.
Write one sentence per line: break after the CJK full stop `。`, the CJK `！`/`？`, and the Latin `.`, `!`, `?`.
You may also break after a clause — `、`, `,`, `;`, `:`, an em dash — when that clarifies structure.
Never wrap prose to a fixed column width, and do not merge a whole paragraph onto one line either: the sentence is the unit.
A line may be any length.
Do not use hard line breaks (two trailing spaces or a trailing backslash) inside a paragraph.
A blank line separates paragraphs.
Rendering is unchanged by all of this, because Markdown joins consecutive lines with a space.

This applies to Markdown files, prose in source-code comments, commit messages, issue and pull-request bodies, and release notes, in English and in Japanese.
In comments, the sentence is still the unit: consecutive comment lines form a paragraph, and a comment trailing after code stands alone.
When you edit a paragraph that is wrapped to a column, reflow the whole paragraph to sentence-per-line in the same change.

The point: rendered output stays identical, while a diff reviews one sentence at a time and a line break means something.
